So the DevTeam thinks gauntlets of fumbling are better than leather
gloves? :) I'm not sure I agree with your logic here.

> Displacement works on nearly all monsters in the early levels
> and on fewer and fewer as you go deeper.  Compare with stealth.
> Stealth works by your sound not reaching distant monsters so
> they can't zero in on you easily.  It also means you do not wake
> up sleeping monsters by walking around or even with combat.
> This makes clearing out a barracks or leprechaun hall a matter
> of killing them one by one in their spot.

You can close off barracks or leprechaun halls without waking anyone up
and come back later with stealth; displacement protects you from turning
a corner or opening a door into a randomly generated awake monster. At
least, it has a chance of protecting you in such a situation, while
stealth does not.

> I have enough problems wwith nymphs that I value stealth over
> displacement in the early game, but let's think about this in
> terms of a ranger.  A ranger is supposed to be a distance fighter
> who avoids getting into melee.  For a ranger, being able to strike
> silently at range should outrank being elusive once you can no
> longer avoid being face to face.

A ranger also gets the least HP per level, sort of (with constitution
bonuses I don't think it works out quite that way, but the base is lower,
at least) and no one can avoid being next to monsters 100% of the time. I
don't mind a nymph being awake if I can pepper it full of arrows; I
find stealth more important when my missile weapons are less powerful,
personally.

> The more I think about this the more I'd want stealth over
> displacement.  And since stealth comes from elven cloak and/or
> elven boots it becomes a choice among more types of armor
> than just cloaks.  Give me elven boots and then I won't mind
> a cloak of displacement over elven cloak.  Until elven boots
> arrive, I would remain tempted to put a [oD into my bag in
> favor of elven cloak.

Even though I would tend to prefer displacement, I think there's
something to this argument; still, I'd rather start with displacement,
since I expect to find another stealth item in normal play much faster
than I expect to find another displacement item. I've gone entire games
without seeing a cloak of displacement; I've never gotten to the Castle
without at least one elven cloak or pair of boots. Stealth also comes
as an intrinsic to some classes --- including rangers at XL 7 --- and
can be prayed for. That's a bit more of a midgame argument though, and I
usually end up ascending with MR or a robe anyway. (I did displacement
recently though, and aside from actually noticing disenchanters and
level drain, it was a lot of fun, and none of the MC-dependent attacks
were that problematic.)

